{"kind":"text","seq":43,"text":"Round 6 is banked at **excitement 7.07**.\n\n**What happened:** I rebuilt the proven skeleton from memory and closed the circuit on the first try (114 pieces, similarity 0.32 — safely under the 0.5 penalty), banking 7.07 immediately. That already beat last round's 6.94.\n\nI then spent the rest of the budget hunting the \"+2 intensity\" the memory playbook predicted would be worth ~7.1+. All three levers failed, and I verified each rather than assuming:\n\n- **360° helices are unbuildable here** — up+down and up+up both rejected as \"in the way\"; 16 units of climb per half-turn isn't enough clearance to stack. Small helices fail the same way.\n- **Unbanked turns did literally nothing** — a geometry-identical swap at two high-speed turns returned a byte-identical eval report. Lateral G was already saturated at 2.80.\n- **The half-loop pair reverses direction**, so it always folds back onto the track it just arrived on. Geometrically it's a perfect drop-in (nets exactly 4 flats), but it collides everywhere there's enough speed to clear its 152-unit climb.\n\nI restored the working tail and re-tested to leave a valid closed circuit standing, confirming 7.07.\n\nTwo things worth flagging honestly: I did **not** beat round 4's all-time 7.13, and the memory's central hypothesis — that intensity ~10.1 is the sweet spot to steer toward — turned out to have no reachable steering mechanism. I've rewritten that memory entry with the three dead ends and a new hypothesis: round 6 beat round 5 by *cutting* ~11 pieces of dead return-leg track, not by adding thrills, so shorter dead track may be the real lever.\n\nOne caveat on the intensity theory itself: it rests on three data points across rounds where the layouts also differed, so the \"9.8–10.2 sweet spot\" may be reading causation into what was really round 4's layout being better. Next round I'd test drop depth (208→288) and dead-track removal directly.\n\n**Best coaster: a 114-piece dual-lift twister with two 208-unit drops, 2 vertical loops and 3 corkscrew inversions — excitement 7.07, intensity 8.08, no crash.**"}
